{"kind":"request","text":"C1: custody record version 2. First build step of the client\ndesign note, ratified by Hugh on 13 November 2026. Read sections\n3, 5 and 12 of `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md` at commit\n847d5542 (branch `planner/client-primitive-note-20261113`).\n\nWork, in `packages/client/src/custody.ts` and each store:\n1. Section 5.1. A record holds the signings of one request. Each\n   outcome gains the index of its signing. A version 1 record\n   reads as version 2 with one signing. `Custody` writes version\n   2 for each new record. Bounds: 8 envelopes and 64 signings.\n   Past them: `full`, reason `signings`, and nothing is signed.\n2. Section 5.2. `send` takes `resign: true`. A caller that does\n   not pass it gets today's behavior.\n3. Section 5.3. `refused` stays terminal for capacity and for\n   sending. It moves to `accepted` on proof. `settle` asks the\n   scope while a signing of a `refused` record is not closed. A\n   `mismatch` to a record with more signings settles the earlier\n   kept envelopes in the same call.\n4. Section 5.4. `folded` and `absent`.\n5. The store port gains the read of the open and returned\n   records of one signer at one target, and the change signal.\n   Each store that implements `CustodyStore` gets both.\n6. A signing is dead by the three rules of section 3. The class\n   `never` comes from RC-1's table.\n\nTests: a literal version 1 record reads as version 2 and\nsettles. Each rule of sections 5.1 to 5.4 has one case. The MCP\nwitnesses of NF-3 keep their behavior, and each takes a new\nliteral for the version 2 bytes, captured one time. Show that\nthe MCP service, the hosted agent and the CLI install change no\nanswer: their tests pass with no edit of an expected answer.\n\nNot in C1: a timer, the replica, the status of a request, a\ndraft.\n\nBefore source edits, send me one act: each store that implements\n`CustodyStore`, the change to each, and each rule of section 5\nthat a store cannot hold as written.\n\